---
import { AWS_GUIDE_PATH, COMPOSE_GUIDE_PATH, HEALTH_EXCERPT } from '@lib/solutions/self-hosting';
---

<section class="border-t border-line" aria-labelledby="sh-health-title">
  <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-24 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 lg:gap-12">
    <div class="lg:col-span-5">
      <h2 id="sh-health-title" class="h2">
        Check it the way the hosted service is checked.
      </h2>
      <ol class="steps-list mt-6 prose">
        <li>
          <strong>Migrations.</strong> They apply before the API starts: Docker Compose runs its <code>migrate</code>{' '}
          job on every start, and the AWS guide runs a one-off migrate task.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Liveness.</strong> <code>/health</code> answers as soon as the process is up. It checks no dependency
          and is never rate limited.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Readiness.</strong> <code>/health/deep</code> checks the database, Redis and the private bucket. Any
          failure answers <code>503</code> with <code>degraded</code>; a bucket not yet configured reads{' '}
          <code>not_configured</code> and does not fail it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-fg-2">
        The readiness check is anonymous, so it is bounded: ten requests a minute per address, one probe at a time, and
        one answer shared for five seconds. Poll it every ten seconds or slower.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="lg:col-span-7">
      <div class="panel sh-term">
        <div class="tabpanel-bar">
          <span class="text-sm font-medium text-fg shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Health checks</span>
          <span class="text-xs text-fg-2">Responses use the API’s one envelope.</span>
        </div>
        <pre class="config" tabindex="0" aria-label="Health check requests and responses" set:text={HEALTH_EXCERPT} />
      </div>

      <div class="rule-list mt-10">
        <div class="py-5">
          <h3 class="h3">Upgrades</h3>
          <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
            The hosted service applies migrations before a new API version takes traffic, and CI checks that every
            schema change ships with its migration and that they apply to an empty database. On your own install, an
            upgrade is a pull of the new source and a rebuild, with the database migrated before the new API starts.
            Take a backup first.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="py-5">
          <h3 class="h3">Backups</h3>
          <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
            Durable state lives in PostgreSQL and the two buckets; Redis holds caches and counters. Keep the encryption
            key with your backups: without it, two-factor and storage secrets cannot be read.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href={`${COMPOSE_GUIDE_PATH}#upgrading`}>
          Upgrades and backups with Docker Compose
          <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
            →
          </span>
        </a>
        <a class="link" href={`${AWS_GUIDE_PATH}#upgrading`}>
          Upgrades and backups on AWS
          <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
            →
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .sh-term {
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .sh-term .config {
    border-radius: 0 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md);
  }
</style>
